        <mxCell id="Rf7NpCmkrSDzaYQikRIU-1" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;fillColor=#DEDEDE;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;Arming PCB diagram:&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;About this diagram :&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Scope : This is the&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;Arming PCB diagram&lt;/u&gt;. It shows the internal logic and wiring of the arming PCB, including shunts and switches. Refer to the global avionics diagram to see how connections are made outside of the arming PCB. Since outside connections are trivial, this diagram is enough to understand the switching and shunting logic.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Goal : The aim is to illustrate how the arming PCB shunts and switches all the connected components. It is just a more readable schematic.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Stage : This diagram depicts both stages&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Switch states : All switches are depicted in their normal (unpressed) state. When an arming rod is inserted and presses on a push-pin, the switch goes into its pressed state (lever connects to the other terminal).&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="245" width="600" x="-370" y="695" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
